INT. MINIVAN - AUTOMATIC CAR WASH - DAY

Dark. Brushes thump the roof. Soap slides down the glass.

ADAEZE OBI, forties, grips the wheel. Eyes ahead. Her husband GREG OBI, forties, in the passenger seat, scrolls his phone. In the back, a TEENAGER in a hoodie stares at the ceiling.

GREG
Two days. That's all I'm saying.

ADAEZE
You've said it four times.

GREG
Because nobody answers.

The brush hits the windshield. Foam. Roar.

ADAEZE
He's right here. Ask him.

GREG
I'm asking you.

The teenager pulls out an earbud.

TEENAGER
I'm not going.

GREG
Nobody asked you.

TEENAGER
You literally just did.

ADAEZE
Don't talk to your father like that.

TEENAGER
Like what? Like I have an opinion?

Greg turns in his seat. Adaeze's knuckles whiten.

GREG
Samir. Look at me.

SAMIR
No.

GREG
Samir.

SAMIR
You're not even driving!

The rollers shriek. The van lurches. Adaeze slams the brake and the car wash stalls with a groan. Water drips. No one speaks.

ADAEZE
(low)
Both of you. Out. Now.

She unbuckles. Opens her door. Steps into the foam.
